\ Euclidean integer division. For `a b`, `a b prim div` is the quotient q and
\ `a b prim mod` the remainder r with a = b*q + r and 0 <= r < |b|: the
\ remainder is never negative, whatever the signs (`-7 2 prim div` is -4 and
\ `-7 2 prim mod` is 1). A zero divisor traps with `primitive-fault` on both
\ hosts. `-9223372036854775808 -1 prim div` is past the signed 64-bit range
\ and traps on the VM, like `prim *`; the reference's integers are unbounded,
\ so the gate reports it as a disagreement rather than a result.

\ Quotient and remainder of `a b`, the remainder on top.
: divmod
  (forall ρ; ρ a:Int^many b:Int^many -- ρ q:Int^many r:Int^many)
  locals { a b } { a b prim div a b prim mod };

: abs
  (forall ρ; ρ n:Int^many -- ρ r:Int^many)
  locals { n } { n 0 prim < [ 0 n prim - ] [ n ] if };

\ Adds the decimal digits of n >= 0 to acc, one `mod 10` and `div 10` a step.
: digit-sum-from
  (forall ρ; ρ n:Int^many acc:Int^many -- ρ r:Int^many)
  locals { n acc }
  { n 0 prim =
    [ acc ]
    [ n 10 prim div acc n 10 prim mod prim + digit-sum-from ]
    if };

\ For n < 0 and d its last decimal digit of |n|: the digit sum of |n|. It
\ never negates n itself, which overflows at -2^63, only n + d, which is a
\ multiple of 10 above -2^63, so |n| div 10 fits in i64.
: digit-sum-negative
  (forall ρ; ρ n:Int^many d:Int^many -- ρ r:Int^many)
  locals { n d } { 0 n d prim + prim - 10 prim div d digit-sum-from };

\ The sum of the decimal digits of |n|, for every i64 n, -2^63 included. For
\ n < 0 the last digit of |n| is (10 - n mod 10) mod 10.
: digit-sum
  (forall ρ; ρ n:Int^many -- ρ r:Int^many)
  locals { n }
  { n 0 prim <
    [ n 10 n 10 prim mod prim - 10 prim mod digit-sum-negative ]
    [ n 0 digit-sum-from ]
    if };

\ The greatest common divisor by Euclid's algorithm: gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b)
\ until b is 0. `mod` is never negative, so the result is too; gcd(0, 0) is 0.
: gcd
  (forall ρ; ρ a:Int^many b:Int^many -- ρ r:Int^many)
  locals { a b } { b 0 prim = [ a abs ] [ b a b prim mod gcd ] if };
